Both halves are fixed and committed as 0890f9fb, with Part of #982 and Part of #1009 in the message. The commit isn't pushed yet; the session's push will take it, and the #982 edit and #1009 comment both cite that SHA.

Before and after (scratch repo, NO_COLOR=1, </dev/null, exit code read directly):

| invocation | before | after |
|---|---|---|
| yoyo blame nosuch.rs | ✗ File not found: nosuch.rs, exit 0 | same text, exit 1 |
| yoyo blame "a b.rs" (committed) | ✗ File not found: "a b.rs", exit 0 | its blame output, exit 0 |
| yoyo blame plain.rs | blame output, exit 0 | byte-identical, exit 0 |

What changed:
• **#1009 (the space):** the `blame` arm in `src/dispatch_sub.rs` now builds its input with the existing `join_args_as_command` instead of `quote_args_as_command`. `/blame` treats everything after it as one path (or path:L-R), the same as the REPL. I didn't reuse find_pattern_from_argv, because it refuses two or more arguments and blame never has. Arguments without spaces join exactly as before, and I checked that plain.rs:1-1 and "a b.rs:1-1" still work.
• **#982 (the exit code):** in `src/commands_git_review.rs`, `handle_blame_status` now returns 1 when the file isn't found. The text is unchanged and still goes to stdout. The REPL `/blame` still ignores the code.
• **One wider change than the task named:** any other git error from blame also exits 1 now. For example, `plain.rs:5-9` on a one-line file used to print git's error and exit 0; now it exits 1. I counted that as "can't blame this file". Running yoyo blame with no file still prints usage and exits 0; that's out of scope.

Verification:
• Three new tests in tests/integration.rs run the built binary:
  • a missing file exits 1;
  • the spaced file prints its blame with no literal " in the output;
  • near miss: the plain file's full stdout matches the step-1 recording exactly (assert_eq!).
• The fixture also checks that a b.rs really is committed and has a space in its name. The existing "outside a repo exits 1" test still passes.
• The near-miss test failed on its first run because I had typed an extra trailing newline into the expected string. I fixed the expectation to match the pre-change recording; the output itself never changed.
• Positive control, run as one mutate→test→restore command: making the missing-file branch return 0 again failed git_blame_of_a_missing_file_exits_one by name, and restoring it went back to green. No marker was left behind.
• `cargo build, cargo test, cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings and cargo fmt -- --check` all pass.

